====================================================================================================
5. #683: how much denser the air at a top gets once the column reads the real
   gravity fall-off; and what a layered tail does to Earth's edge
====================================================================================================
  An isothermal column under g(z) = g R^2/(R+z)^2 integrates to
  p = p0 exp[-(R/H) z/(R+z)], so the flat model's density at altitude z is
  light by exp[(z/H) z/(R+z)] -- H at the datum, z just under the top. Small
  for a big world, enormous for a small one.
  sys    body         R km   H_eff m   top km        gain
  solar  Jupiter     69911     27000      270       1.04x
  solar  Uranus      25362     20000      200       1.08x
  solar  Neptune     24622     20000      200       1.08x
  solar  Saturn      58232     60000      600       1.11x
  solar  Earth        6371      8423       85       1.14x
  solar  Venus        6052     16000      160       1.29x
  ksp_s  Jool         6000     20000      200       1.38x
  solar  Mars         3390     11500      115       1.39x
  solar  Titan        2575     20000      200       2.06x
  ksp_s  Laythe        500      5500       50       2.29x
  ksp_s  Shay          550      6000       60       2.67x
  ksp_s  Kerbin        600      5500       70       3.78x
  ksp_s  Eve           700      7000       90       4.33x
  ksp_s  Duna          320      4000       50       5.41x

  Earth's authored 7-level table vs an exponential with the SAME datum and
  the SAME 85 km top, at 84,999 m:
    layered   p     0.3573 Pa  rho  6.669e-06
    exp H 8423 p      4.199 Pa  rho  5.076e-05   -> layered is 11.8x thinner in p, 7.6x in rho

  And the Kerbin option C warning: Earth's table copied VERBATIM onto Kerbin
  (height 70 km) is refused at load -- atmo.h:131 needs L.back().base_alt < top,
  and Earth's last level is based at 71 km. C above is Earth's first SIX levels;
  the 71 km row never governs below 70 km, so dropping it changes nothing.

====================================================================================================
6. what a 288 K Kerbin costs the player, and where its top has to sit to stay
   boost-free at the edge (the #686 x #682 coupling)
====================================================================================================
  Density and circular-orbit q at the altitude players actually use. Baseline is
  what ships today (option A: H 5500, top 70 km).
  alt m          A rho / q    C top 70 km   C top 100 km
  20000     3.23e-02 91924.9Pa 8.79e-02 250477.6Pa 8.79e-02 250477.6Pa
  40000     8.50e-04 2346.3Pa 3.84e-03 10603.6Pa 3.84e-03 10603.6Pa
  60000     2.24e-05  59.9Pa 2.87e-04 769.1Pa 2.87e-04 769.1Pa
  69999     3.64e-06   9.6Pa 7.40e-05 195.0Pa 7.40e-05 195.0Pa
  84999     0.00e+00   0.0Pa 0.00e+00   0.0Pa 6.68e-06  17.2Pa
  99999     0.00e+00   0.0Pa 0.00e+00   0.0Pa 3.11e-07   0.8Pa

  Where option C's edge drag EQUALS today's edge drag (q 9.6 Pa at circular):
    top = 88 km puts the edge at q 9.6 Pa, rho 3.74e-06 (= rho0 x 3.1e-06)
    T there: 166.6 K.  Ten datum scale heights would be 84 km, so the layered
    tail needs slightly MORE than 10H to thin to the same q as today.

  What a 'computed top' really means, per body: top = 10 x H (today's rule when
  no height is authored) always puts the edge at rho/rho0 = e^-10 = 4.5e-5. The
  number drag actually cares about is the ABSOLUTE edge density and the q it gives
  at that body's circular speed -- and those differ by seven orders of magnitude.
  sys    body        top km   edge rho    q at circ     rho/rho0      10H km
  solar  Venus          160   2.95e-03      77168.7     4.54e-05         160
  solar  Titan          200   2.41e-04        389.3     4.54e-05         200
  ksp_s  Laythe          50   1.38e-04        246.3     1.13e-04          55
  ksp_s  Jool           200   9.08e-05       2068.9     4.54e-05         200
  ksp_s  Shay            60   5.56e-05        124.0     4.54e-05          60
  solar  Neptune        200   4.09e-05       5625.7     4.54e-05         200
  solar  Uranus         200   2.72e-05       3087.2     4.54e-05         200
  solar  Earth           85   6.67e-06        205.9     5.44e-06          84
  solar  Jupiter        270   7.26e-06       6556.2     4.54e-05         270
  ksp_s  Eve             90   4.43e-06         22.9     2.61e-06          70
  ksp_s  Kerbin          70   3.64e-06          9.6     2.97e-06          55
  solar  Saturn         600   4.54e-06       1463.5     4.54e-05         600
  solar  Mars           115   9.08e-07          5.6     4.54e-05         115
  ksp_s  Duna            50   4.47e-07          0.2     3.73e-06          40

  Earth's authored 1976 levels put a hard ceiling on their own `height`, and it is
  a LOAD refusal rather than a silent vacuum: resolveAtmoLevels (atmo.h:141-145)
  demands T > 0 at every level's ceiling, so a cooling last level bounds the top at
  base_alt + base_T / |lapse| no matter what the file types.
    top  85 km: loads    T   186.7 K  rho  6.67e-06  q at circular     205.9 Pa  shell   102 km
    top 100 km: loads    T   156.7 K  rho  3.97e-07  q at circular      12.2 Pa  shell   120 km
    top 120 km: loads    T   116.7 K  rho  3.44e-09  q at circular       0.1 Pa  shell   144 km
    top 150 km: loads    T    56.7 K  rho  3.05e-14  q at circular       0.0 Pa  shell   180 km
    top 179 km: REFUSED  T     0.0 K  rho  0.00e+00  q at circular       0.0 Pa  shell   215 km
    top 200 km: REFUSED  T     0.0 K  rho  0.00e+00  q at circular       0.0 Pa  shell   240 km
    -> Earth's 7 levels top out at 178.3 km (the 71 km level: 214.65 K / 0.002 K/m).
       Kerbin's six-level option tops out at 147.7 km (the 51 km level: 270.65 K,
       lapse -0.0028). A 200 km Earth needs a NEW level, and the real thermosphere
       up there is HOT and rising -- i.e. thicker air, which is exactly the drag we
       are choosing not to simulate.

  Kerbin, the same six-level table, edge numbers per candidate top:
    top  70 km: loads    q at circular   195.0 Pa  rho  7.40e-05  shell   100 km
    top  80 km: loads    q at circular    41.0 Pa  rho  1.58e-05  shell   100 km
    top  85 km: loads    q at circular    17.2 Pa  rho  6.68e-06  shell   102 km
    top  88 km: loads    q at circular     9.9 Pa  rho  3.86e-06  shell   106 km
    top 100 km: loads    q at circular     0.8 Pa  rho  3.11e-07  shell   120 km
    top 120 km: loads    q at circular     0.0 Pa  rho  7.01e-10  shell   144 km
    top 150 km: REFUSED  q at circular     0.0 Pa  rho  0.00e+00  shell   180 km

  ... and the same table with its DENSITY scaled, to ask whether a warm Kerbin
  could keep today's edge density at all. Today rho(70 km) = 3.64e-06.
    rho0  1.2250 -> p0    101325 Pa (100.0 % of Earth), rho(70 km)  7.40e-05, q   195.0 Pa
    rho0  0.6125 -> p0     50662 Pa (50.0 % of Earth), rho(70 km)  3.70e-05, q    97.5 Pa
    rho0  0.1225 -> p0     10132 Pa (10.0 % of Earth), rho(70 km)  7.40e-06, q    19.5 Pa
    rho0  0.0602 -> p0      4981 Pa ( 4.9 % of Earth), rho(70 km)  3.64e-06, q     9.6 Pa
    -> holding today's edge density at a 288 K datum needs rho0 0.0602, i.e. a
       4981 Pa surface (4.9 % of Earth): a warm Kerbin with today's low-orbit
       drag budget is a near-vacuum world. The datum and the flight envelope are
       one column, not two knobs.

  Orbital decay, for scale: a 2 m capsule (capsule in res/data/parts.json:
  mass 1149.82 kg, radius 1.0 m -> ~3.14 m^2 broadside, drag_forward 0.35).
      70.0 km  q     9.6 Pa  a 0.0092 m/s^2  tau   250398 s = 136.6 orbits
      70.0 km  q   195.0 Pa  a 0.1865 m/s^2  tau    12310 s =   6.7 orbits
      85.0 km  q    17.2 Pa  a 0.0165 m/s^2  tau   137887 s =  72.7 orbits
      88.0 km  q     9.9 Pa  a 0.0095 m/s^2  tau   239146 s = 125.3 orbits

  Frame cost of moving a top (bodylimits.h:34, shellEdge = max(100 km, 1.2 x top)):
    top  70 km -> shellEdge   100 km, LowOrbit band 14 km wide
    top  85 km -> shellEdge   102 km, LowOrbit band 17 km wide
    top 100 km -> shellEdge   120 km, LowOrbit band 20 km wide
    top 120 km -> shellEdge   144 km, LowOrbit band 24 km wide

shellEdge law (bodylimits.h): max(100 km, 1.2 x top) -> 100 km for a 70 km top
